.TH GETOPT 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
getopt \- parse command-line options for shell scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B getopt
.B \-o
.I optstring
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ parameter ...]
.br
.B getopt
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I optstring
.RI [ parameter ...]
.br
.B getopt
.I optstring
.RI [ parameter ...]
.br
.B getopt \-T
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B getopt
sorts its
.I parameters
into options and operands, and writes them on standard output in a fixed
form.
A shell script passes its own arguments, then hands the output back to the
shell with
.BR "eval set \-\-" ,
so that a simple loop can read the options; see
.BR EXAMPLES .
.PP
.I optstring
lists the short options to recognize, and
.B \-l
lists the long options.
Without
.BR \-o ,
.I optstring
is the first argument after the options of
.B getopt
itself.
Those options end at the first argument that is not an option, or at
.BR \-\- .
Every later argument is
.I optstring
or a parameter, even if it begins with a dash.
If the first argument after the options begins with a dash, precede it
with
.BR \-\- .
When the first argument is not an option or
.BR \-\- ,
as in
.BR "getopt ab \-a" ,
the traditional form described below applies instead.
.SS Option strings
Each letter of
.I optstring
is a short option.
A letter followed by a colon takes an argument, which is attached to the
option, as in
.BI \-b value\fR,
or is the next parameter.
A letter followed by two colons takes an optional argument, which must be
attached.
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-ab ,
and
.B \-\-
ends the options.
A lone
.B \-
is an operand.
.PP
Unless told otherwise, options and operands may be mixed, and the output
lists every operand after the options.
These characters at the start of
.I optstring
change the scan:
.TP
.B +
Stop at the first operand.
It and everything after it are operands.
Setting the environment variable
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
has the same effect, unless
.I optstring
starts with
.BR \- .
.TP
.B \-
Do not move operands to the end.
Each operand appears in its place among the options, as a quoted argument.
.TP
.B :
Print no error messages, as with
.BR \-q .
It must follow any
.B +
or
.BR \- .
.PP
An unknown or ambiguous option, a missing argument, or an argument given
to a long option that takes none is reported on standard error, left out
of the output, and makes the exit status 1.
Scanning continues after it.
.SS Long options
.B \-l
gives a list of long option names, separated by commas or white space.
A name followed by a colon takes an argument, and a name followed by two
colons takes an optional argument.
.B \-l
may be repeated, and the lists add up.
.PP
On the command line a long option is written
.BI \-\- name\fR.
A required argument follows an equals sign, as in
.BI \-\- name = value\fR,
or is the next parameter.
An optional argument can only follow an equals sign.
A name may be shortened to a prefix, and the output has the full name.
An exact match always wins.
A prefix of several names is ambiguous only when they differ in whether
they take an argument; otherwise the first of them in the order given to
.B \-l
is used.
.PP
With
.BR \-a ,
a long option may also start with a single dash.
Such an argument, or the rest of a group after a short option, is read
as a long option if it matches one, unless it is a single letter that is
also a short option.
Otherwise its first letter is read as a short option, or, if it is not
one, the whole remainder is reported as an unknown option.
.SS Output
Each item of the output is preceded by a space, and the output ends with
a line feed.
The items are the options in the order given, each as
.BI \- c
or
.BI \-\- name
(the full name of a long option), followed by its argument if it takes one.
An optional argument that is absent is written as an empty argument.
Then comes
.BR \-\- ,
then the operands.
.PP
Each option argument and each operand is enclosed in single quotes, and
each single quote inside it is written as
.BR \(aq\e\(aq\(aq ,
so that the shell reads back the original string.
A newline inside an argument is written as it is.
.B \-u
turns quoting off.
With
.B \-s csh
or
.BR "\-s tcsh" ,
each exclamation mark and each white-space character other than newline is
also written as a closing quote, a backslash, the character, and an opening
quote, and a newline is written as a backslash and the letter
.BR n .
.SS Traditional form
If the first argument does not begin with a dash, or if
.B GETOPT_COMPATIBLE
is set, the first argument is
.I optstring
and all the rest are parameters, and no option of
.B getopt
is recognized.
There are no long options, and the output is not quoted, as with
.BR \-u .
Leading
.B +
and
.B \-
characters of
.I optstring
are ignored.
Error messages carry the name the program was started under.
With no arguments at all, it reports a missing
.IR optstring ,
except that when
.B GETOPT_COMPATIBLE
is set it writes the output for no parameters, which is just
.BR \-\- ,
and exits 0.
.SH OPTIONS
These are the options of
.B getopt
itself.
They may be grouped, as in
.BR \-uq .
A short option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-oab ,
or be the next argument.
A long option's argument follows an equals sign or is the next argument.
Long options may be shortened to a unique prefix.
An error in these options prints a message and the usage text on
standard error, and exits with status 1.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-alternative\fR
Allow long options to start with a single dash.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-longoptions\fR \fIlongopts\fR
Recognize the long options in
.IR longopts .
May be repeated.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR
Use
.I name
as the program name in the error messages about the parameters.
The default is the name the program was started under, normally
.BR getopt .
Messages about the command line of
.B getopt
itself are not affected.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-options\fR \fIoptstring\fR
Recognize the short options in
.IR optstring .
Without
.BR \-o ,
the first operand is
.IR optstring .
If repeated, the last one wins.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print no error messages about the parameters.
An error still makes the exit status 1.
Errors in the command line of
.B getopt
itself are still reported.
.TP
.BR \-Q ", " \-\-quiet\-output
Write nothing on standard output.
Use it to test parameters by the exit status alone.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-shell\fR \fIshell\fR
Set the quoting convention to that of
.IR shell ,
which is
.B bash
or
.B sh
(the default),
.BR csh ,
or
.BR tcsh .
The names are case-sensitive.
Any other name produces a warning on standard error and is treated as
.BR bash .
Has no effect with
.BR \-u .
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-test
Exit with status 4 without writing anything, as a test for this
.BR getopt .
The other options are still checked first.
When
.B GETOPT_COMPATIBLE
is set,
.B \-T
is the
.I optstring
of the traditional form instead, so the output is
.B \-\-
and the exit status 0.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unquoted
Do not quote the output.
An empty argument, or one with white space or characters special to the
shell, then does not survive the shell's word splitting.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and exit
with status 0.
There is no
.B \-h
or
.BR \-V .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Read when
.B busybox.exe
starts.
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime expands wildcards in the command-line arguments before
.B getopt
sees them.
If it is unset, startup sets it to
.B 0
in the environment of child processes, so that they do not expand wildcards
again.
.TP
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ", " BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION
When standard output is a console, decide whether the console or busybox
interprets escape sequences in the output.
See
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B CONEMUPID
When it is set and neither of the above is, busybox uses terminal mode
0: it turns virtual terminal output off and emulates escape sequences
itself.
.TP
.B GETOPT_COMPATIBLE
If set, even to an empty value, use the traditional form.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, even to an empty value, stop scanning the parameters at the first
operand, as with a leading
.B +
in
.IR optstring .
It has no effect when
.I optstring
starts with
.BR \- .
The C runtime reads it.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The parameters contained an error, or the command line of
.B getopt
is invalid: an unknown or ambiguous option, a missing option argument or
.IR optstring ,
or an empty long option name.
.TP
.B 4
.B \-T
was given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read the options of a script.
The loop ends at the
.B \-\-
that
.B getopt
writes after the options:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat opts.sh
args=$(getopt \-n opts \-o vo: \-l verbose,output: \-\- "$@") || exit 1
eval set \-\- "$args"
while :; do
    case $1 in
    \-v|\-\-verbose) verbose=1; shift;;
    \-o|\-\-output)  output=$2; shift 2;;
    \-\-)           shift; break;;
    esac
done
echo "verbose=$verbose output=$output files: $*"
$ sh opts.sh \-v \-\-out=a.txt "my file" b.c
verbose=1 output=a.txt files: my file b.c
$ sh opts.sh \-x
opts: unknown option \-\- x
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show what
.B getopt
writes for a short option, a short option with an attached argument, a
long option with an optional argument, and operands, one of which holds a
quote:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ getopt \-o ab: \-l long:: \-\- \-a \-bfoo \-\-long=x "it\(aqs" rest
 \-a \-b \(aqfoo\(aq \-\-long \(aqx\(aq \-\- \(aqit\(aq\e\(aq\(aqs\(aq \(aqrest\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check parameters without writing anything or printing errors:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ getopt \-qQ \-o ab \-\- \-a \-z || echo bad
bad
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the traditional form, whose output is not quoted:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ set \-\- \-a x
$ set \-\- $(getopt ab "$@")
$ echo "$@"
\-a \-\- x
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The parameters are scanned by the getopt functions of the mingw-w64 C
runtime, not by the GNU C library, so some details differ from util-linux
.BR getopt :
.IP \(bu 2
The error messages are the runtime's, such as
.BR "getopt: unknown option \-\- x" ,
.BR "getopt: option requires an argument \-\- b" ,
.BR "getopt: option doesn\(aqt take an argument \-\- alpha" ,
and
.BR "getopt: ambiguous option \-\- al" .
.IP \(bu 2
A prefix that matches several long options is an error only when they
differ in whether they take an argument; otherwise the first of them is
used.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-a ,
the rest of a group after a short option may be read as a long option.
.IP \(bu 2
Every failure exits with status 1, where util-linux also uses 2 and 3.
.PP
The output ends in a line feed, not CRLF.
Arguments are copied byte for byte, and paths are not converted: drive
letters and backslashes stay as typed, and the single quotes keep
backslashes literal for the shell.
When standard output is a console, the output is converted for display
from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and escape sequences in it take effect.
Only a POSIX shell such as
.BR sh (1)
can read the quoted output, not
.BR cmd.exe .
The shell builtin
.BR getopts ,
also described in
.BR sh (1),
needs no external program but has no long options.
.PP
When started from outside the kit shell, as from
.BR cmd.exe ,
the program expands wildcards in its arguments first; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1)
